#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175749 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f14ba254-fe70-511f-aad9-2903d4d6fe1e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Surface Area of Revolution"}] :question/id #uuid "6a090caa-4c73-5f90-9cb1-dc7dcdfe9e37" :question/math-academy-id "e-8906" :question/problem "Consider the segment of the line $y = 2x$ that lies between the lines $x = 0$ and $x = 2$. Calculate the surface area generated when this segment is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi x\\,\\textrm d s\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y = f(x)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} x\\sqrt{1 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\right)^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(2x) = 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\right)^2 = 4\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= 2π∫_{x_{1}}^{x_{2}}x\\sqrt{1 + (\\frac{dy}{dx})^{2}}dx \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{2}x\\sqrt{1 + 4}dx \\\\\n&= 2π\\sqrt{5}∫_{0}^{2}xdx \\\\\n&= 2π\\sqrt{5}[\\frac{x^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= 2π\\sqrt{5}[\\frac{2^{2}}{2} - \\frac{0^{2}}{2}] \\\\\n&= 4π\\sqrt{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175750 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bff48a7c-66d6-55e8-99a1-ae8c80925335" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of Y"}] :question/id #uuid "6ee942e8-9fb9-5dfb-8f9c-aea1dd6d53ea" :question/math-academy-id "e-8907" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $x=y^2$ that lies between $y=0$ and $y=2$, as shown below. What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3632/Source/Images/99e6227dbd52eb759c011421688d7d6f.png)" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi x\\,\\textrm d s\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x = f(y)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d y}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d y\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{y_1}^{y_2} x \\sqrt{1+ \\left(\\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}y\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=2$.\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dy} &= \\frac{d}{dy}(y^{2}) = 2y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= 2π∫_{y_{1}}^{y_{2}}x\\sqrt{1 + (\\frac{dx}{dy})^{2}}dy \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{2}(y^{2})\\sqrt{1 + (2y)^{2}}dy \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{2}y^{2}\\sqrt{1 + 4y^{2}}dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175765 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7cf4318c-a323-5224-92df-879ebc2bf3b0" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of X"}] :question/id #uuid "d0546997-c726-5ec2-ae10-c2373ed8b863" :question/math-academy-id "e-9121" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=x^3-3x$ that lies in the fourth quadrant between its $x$-intercepts. What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3632/Source/Images/da72f86944875b96d066b7c3305ca7e0.png)" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi x\\,\\textrm d s\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y = f(x)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} x \\sqrt{1+ \\left(\\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{3} - 3x &= 0 \\\\\nx(x^{2} - 3) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 0, ± \\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to\n\n$$\nx= \\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{3} - 3x) = 3x^{2} - 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= 2π∫_{x_{1}}^{x_{2}}x\\sqrt{1 + (\\frac{dy}{dx})^{2}}dx \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}x\\sqrt{1 + (3x^{2} - 3)^{2}}dx \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}x\\sqrt{9x^{4} - 18x^{2} + 10}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176130 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7cf4318c-a323-5224-92df-879ebc2bf3b0" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of X"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a749ded0-2fb8-5d57-a65d-979e2ef8edd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π∫_{0}^{π/2}\\sin x\\sqrt{1 + \\cos^{2} x}dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173443} {:answer/id #uuid "f402d949-5cd4-56b1-bd76-5a119f45abc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π∫_{0}^{π/2}x\\cos^{2} xdx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173444} {:answer/id #uuid "0538f271-9c1d-52ab-af5c-03d48163d2c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π∫_{0}^{π/2}\\sin x\\sqrt{1 + \\sin^{2} x}dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173445} {:answer/id #uuid "077afecb-b76b-5b22-822e-052d0c159b2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π∫_{0}^{π/2}x\\sin^{2} xdx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173446} {:answer/id #uu